<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--  SOCRATIC DERIVATION                                         -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<section>
  <h2>1. Socratic Derivation: v = &plusmn;&omega;&radic;(x&#8320;&sup2; &minus; x&sup2;)</h2>
  <p>We shall derive the velocity&ndash;displacement relation for a particle executing SHM using the principle of conservation of mechanical energy. Consider a mass&ndash;spring system with spring constant <em>k</em> and mass <em>m</em>, oscillating with amplitude <em>x</em><sub>0</sub>.</p>

  <div class="derivation-step">
    <span class="step-num">Step 1</span>
    <span class="prompt">&#9654; Write the total mechanical energy of the system at an arbitrary displacement <em>x</em> where the particle has velocity <em>v</em>.</span>
    <span class="result">E<sub>total</sub> = &frac12;mv&sup2; + &frac12;kx&sup2;</span>
    <span style="display:block;font-size:0.85rem;color:#636c76;">The total energy is the sum of kinetic energy (&frac12;mv&sup2;) and elastic potential energy (&frac12;kx&sup2;).</span>
  </div>

  <div class="derivation-step">
    <span class="step-num">Step 2</span>
    <span class="prompt">&#9654; What is the velocity at the maximum displacement <em>x</em> = <em>x</em><sub>0</sub>? Hence write E<sub>total</sub> in terms of <em>k</em> and <em>x</em><sub>0</sub> only.</span>
    <span class="result">At x = x<sub>0</sub>, v = 0 &nbsp;&rArr;&nbsp; E<sub>total</sub> = &frac12;kx<sub>0</sub>&sup2;</span>
    <span style="display:block;font-size:0.85rem;color:#636c76;">At the turning points the particle is momentarily at rest; all energy is stored as potential energy in the spring.</span>
  </div>

  <div class="derivation-step">
    <span class="step-num">Step 3</span>
    <span class="prompt">&#9654; Energy is conserved. Equate the two expressions for E<sub>total</sub>.</span>
    <span class="result">&frac12;mv&sup2; + &frac12;kx&sup2; = &frac12;kx<sub>0</sub>&sup2;</span>
    <span style="display:block;font-size:0.85rem;color:#636c76;">No energy enters or leaves the system &mdash; mechanical energy is strictly conserved in ideal SHM.</span>
  </div>

  <div class="derivation-step">
    <span class="step-num">Step 4</span>
    <span class="prompt">&#9654; Multiply through by 2 and rearrange to isolate the term containing v&sup2;.</span>
    <span class="result">mv&sup2; = kx<sub>0</sub>&sup2; &minus; kx&sup2; = k(x<sub>0</sub>&sup2; &minus; x&sup2;)</span>
    <span style="display:block;font-size:0.85rem;color:#636c76;">The kinetic energy term is now isolated. Divide by <em>m</em> to obtain v&sup2;.</span>
  </div>

  <div class="derivation-step">
    <span class="step-num">Step 5</span>
    <span class="prompt">&#9654; Divide by <em>m</em> to obtain an expression for v&sup2;.</span>
    <span class="result">v&sup2; = (k/m)(x<sub>0</sub>&sup2; &minus; x&sup2;)</span>
    <span style="display:block;font-size:0.85rem;color:#636c76;">The ratio <em>k/m</em> governs the stiffness-to-inertia balance of the oscillator.</span>
  </div>

  <div class="derivation-step">
    <span class="step-num">Step 6</span>
    <span class="prompt">&#9654; Recall the defining relation for angular frequency in SHM: &omega;&sup2; = k/m. Substitute and take the square root.</span>
    <span class="result">v&sup2; = &omega;&sup2;(x<sub>0</sub>&sup2; &minus; x&sup2;) &nbsp;&rArr;&nbsp; v = &plusmn;&omega;&radic;(x<sub>0</sub>&sup2; &minus; x&sup2;)</span>
    <span style="display:block;font-size:0.85rem;color:#636c76;">The &plusmn; sign indicates that the particle may be travelling in either direction (towards or away from equilibrium) at a given displacement.</span>
  </div>

  <div class="final-formula">
    v = &plusmn;&omega;&radic;(x<sub>0</sub>&sup2; &minus; x&sup2;)
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--  SHM DEFINITION                                             -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<section>
  <h2>2. Definition and Conditions for Simple Harmonic Motion</h2>
  <p>Simple harmonic motion is defined as an oscillation in which the acceleration <em>a</em> is directly proportional to the displacement <em>x</em> from a fixed point and is always directed towards that point.</p>

  <div class="final-formula">a = &minus;&omega;&sup2;x</div>

  <p>where &omega; is the angular frequency (measured in rad s<sup>&minus;1</sup>). The negative sign indicates that the acceleration is always directed towards the equilibrium position (restoring). Equivalently:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Restoring force: <strong>F = &minus;kx</strong> &nbsp;(where k = m&omega;&sup2; is the force constant)</li>
    <li>Condition for SHM: <strong>a &prop; &minus;x</strong></li>
    <li>Angular frequency: <strong>&omega; = 2&pi;f = 2&pi;/T</strong></li>
  </ul>

  <p>For a body to execute SHM, the restoring force must obey Hooke's law (F = &minus;kx) over the range of motion.</p>
</section>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--  KINEMATICS OF SHM                                           -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<section>
  <h2>3. Kinematics of Simple Harmonic Motion</h2>

  <p>The displacement of a particle executing SHM can be described by a sinusoidal function of time:</p>

  <div class="final-formula">x = x<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t) &nbsp; or &nbsp; x = x<sub>0</sub> cos(&omega;t)</div>

  <p>Differentiating with respect to time gives expressions for velocity and acceleration:</p>

  <table class="data-table" style="min-width:75%;">
    <tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Expression</th><th>Phase relative to x</th></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Displacement</strong></td><td>x = x<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t)</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Velocity</strong></td><td>v = &omega;x<sub>0</sub> cos(&omega;t) = &omega;x<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t + &pi;/2)</td><td>Leads displacement by &pi;/2 (T/4)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Acceleration</strong></td><td>a = &minus;&omega;&sup2;x<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t) = &omega;&sup2;x<sub>0</sub> sin(&omega;t + &pi;)</td><td>Opposes displacement (phase diff. &pi;)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Max. speed</strong></td><td>v<sub>max</sub> = &omega;x<sub>0</sub></td><td>At equilibrium (x = 0)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Max. acceleration</strong></td><td>a<sub>max</sub> = &omega;&sup2;x<sub>0</sub></td><td>At maximum displacement (x = &plusmn;x<sub>0</sub>)</td></tr>
  </table>

  <div class="graph-container">
    {{GRAPH_PHASE_RELATIONS}}
    <p class="caption">Fig. 7: Displacement, velocity and acceleration as functions of time. Note the phase differences: velocity leads displacement by T/4, acceleration opposes displacement (antiphase).</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--  PERIOD OF OSCILLATION                                       -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<section>
  <h2>4. Period of Oscillation for Common SHM Systems</h2>

  <h3>4.1 Mass&ndash;Spring System</h3>
  <p>The period of a mass&ndash;spring oscillator depends only on the mass <em>m</em> and the spring constant <em>k</em>. Let us derive <strong>T = 2&pi;&radic;(m/k)</strong> step by step.</p>

  <div class="derivation-step">
    <span class="step-num">Step 1</span>
    <span class="prompt">&#9654; Write the restoring force on a mass <em>m</em> attached to a spring of force constant <em>k</em> at displacement <em>x</em>.</span>
    <span class="result">F = &minus;kx</span>
    <span style="display:block;font-size:0.85rem;color:#636c76;">Hooke's law: the spring exerts a restoring force proportional to displacement and directed towards equilibrium.</span>
  </div>

  <div class="derivation-step">
    <span class="step-num">Step 2</span>
    <span class="prompt">&#9654; Apply Newton's second law: F = ma. Substitute the restoring force.</span>
    <span class="result">ma = &minus;kx &nbsp;&rArr;&nbsp; a = &minus;(k/m)x</span>
    <span style="display:block;font-size:0.85rem;color:#636c76;">The acceleration is proportional to &minus;x with constant of proportionality (k/m).</span>
  </div>

  <div class="derivation-step">
    <span class="step-num">Step 3</span>
    <span class="prompt">&#9654; Recall the defining equation for SHM: a = &minus;&omega;&sup2;x. Compare with Step 2 to identify &omega;.</span>
    <span class="result">&omega;&sup2; = k/m &nbsp;&rArr;&nbsp; &omega; = &radic;(k/m)</span>
    <span style="display:block;font-size:0.85rem;color:#636c76;">The angular frequency is determined by the stiffness-to-mass ratio. A stiffer spring (larger <em>k</em>) or a lighter mass gives a higher frequency.</span>
  </div>

  <div class="derivation-step">
    <span class="step-num">Step 4</span>
    <span class="prompt">&#9654; Use the relation between angular frequency and period: &omega; = 2&pi;/T. Substitute for &omega;.</span>
    <span class="result">2&pi;/T = &radic;(k/m) &nbsp;&rArr;&nbsp; T = 2&pi;&radic;(m/k)</span>
    <span style="display:block;font-size:0.85rem;color:#636c76;"><strong>Conceptual insight:</strong> Larger mass &rarr; more inertia &rarr; slower oscillation. Larger <em>k</em> &rarr; stiffer spring &rarr; faster oscillation. The period does NOT depend on amplitude &mdash; this is the property of <strong>isochronism</strong>.</span>
  </div>

  <div class="final-formula" style="font-size:1.05rem;padding:0.3cm 0.5cm;">
    T = 2&pi;&radic;(m/k) &nbsp;&nbsp; <span style="font-size:0.9rem;font-weight:400;color:#2b6f9e;">Mass&ndash;spring period &#8212; independent of g and amplitude</span>
  </div>

  <h3>4.2 Simple Pendulum (small-angle approximation)</h3>
  <p>For a pendulum bob of mass <em>m</em> on a string of length <em>L</em>, the restoring force is gravity. Let us derive <strong>T = 2&pi;&radic;(L/g)</strong>.</p>

  <div class="derivation-step">
    <span class="step-num">Step 1</span>
    <span class="prompt">&#9654; Consider the pendulum displaced by a small angle &theta; from equilibrium. Resolve the weight mg into components. What is the restoring force?</span>
    <span class="result">Restoring force = &minus;mg sin&theta; &nbsp; (the component perpendicular to the string)</span>
    <span style="display:block;font-size:0.85rem;color:#636c76;">The tension in the string balances mg cos&theta;. The net restoring force comes from the tangential component of weight.</span>
  </div>

  <div class="derivation-step">
    <span class="step-num">Step 2</span>
    <span class="prompt">&#9654; For small angles, sin&theta; &asymp; &theta; (in radians). Also relate the arc displacement <em>x</em> to &theta;: x = L&theta;.</span>
    <span class="result">F &asymp; &minus;mg&theta; = &minus;mg(x/L) = &minus;(mg/L)x</span>
    <span style="display:block;font-size:0.85rem;color:#636c76;"><strong>Critical approximation:</strong> For &theta; &lt; 10&deg; (0.17 rad), sin&theta; differs from &theta; by less than 0.5%. Without this approximation, the motion would not be simple harmonic.</span>
  </div>

  <div class="derivation-step">
    <span class="step-num">Step 3</span>
    <span class="prompt">&#9654; Apply Newton's second law: F = ma. Use the expression for F from Step 2.</span>
    <span class="result">ma = &minus;(mg/L)x &nbsp;&rArr;&nbsp; a = &minus;(g/L)x</span>
    <span style="display:block;font-size:0.85rem;color:#636c76;">Comparing with a = &minus;&omega;&sup2;x, we see that (g/L) plays the role of &omega;&sup2;. The mass <em>m</em> cancels out!</span>
  </div>

  <div class="derivation-step">
    <span class="step-num">Step 4</span>
    <span class="prompt">&#9654; Identify &omega; and then derive T using &omega; = 2&pi;/T.</span>
    <span class="result">&omega;&sup2; = g/L &nbsp;&rArr;&nbsp; &omega; = &radic;(g/L) &nbsp;&rArr;&nbsp; T = 2&pi;&radic;(L/g)</span>
    <span style="display:block;font-size:0.85rem;color:#636c76;"><strong>Conceptual insights:</strong> The period is independent of mass (m cancels). Longer pendulum &rarr; larger period. Weaker gravity (smaller g) &rarr; larger period. This formula enables accurate measurement of g by timing a pendulum.</span>
  </div>

  <div class="final-formula" style="font-size:1.05rem;padding:0.3cm 0.5cm;">
    T = 2&pi;&radic;(L/g) &nbsp;&nbsp; <span style="font-size:0.9rem;font-weight:400;color:#2b6f9e;">Pendulum period &#8212; independent of mass and amplitude (small angles only)</span>
  </div>

  <h3>4.3 Comparison Summary</h3>
  <table class="data-table" style="min-width:80%;">
    <tr><th>Property</th><th>Mass&ndash;Spring</th><th>Simple Pendulum</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Period</td><td>T = 2&pi;&radic;(m/k)</td><td>T = 2&pi;&radic;(L/g)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Restoring mechanism</td><td>Elastic (spring)</td><td>Gravitational (weight component)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Depends on mass?</td><td>Yes (T &prop; &radic;m)</td><td>No (mass cancels out)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Depends on gravity?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (T &prop; 1/&radic;g)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Amplitude limit?</td><td>Ideal spring: any amplitude (Hooke's law limit)</td><td>Small angles only (&theta; &lt; 10&deg; for &lt; 0.5% error)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Applications</td><td>Suspension systems, seismometers, clock mechanisms</td><td>Pendulum clocks, measuring g, metronomes</td></tr>
  </table>
</section>

