      {{GRAPH_ENERGY_DISPLACEMENT}}
      <p class="caption">Fig. 1: Energy vs displacement. E<sub>p</sub> follows a parabolic curve, E<sub>k</sub> is an inverted parabola, and E<sub>total</sub> remains constant.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="graph-container">
      {{GRAPH_ENERGY_TIME}}
      <p class="caption">Fig. 2: Energy vs time. E<sub>p</sub> and E<sub>k</sub> oscillate at twice the frequency of the displacement, with a phase difference of &frac14;T between maxima.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="graph-container">
      {{GRAPH_VELOCITY_DISPLACEMENT}}
      <p class="caption">Fig. 3: Velocity vs displacement. The elliptical locus confirms the relation v = &plusmn;&omega;&radic;(x<sub>0</sub>&sup2; &minus; x&sup2;). The arrow indicates the direction of motion.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="graph-container">
      {{GRAPH_DAMPED_ENERGY}}
      <p class="caption">Fig. 4: Damped oscillation. The total energy envelope decays exponentially as E(t) = E<sub>0</sub>e<sup>&minus;&gamma;t</sup>. The instantaneous energy oscillates within this envelope.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="graph-container">
      {{GRAPH_HOTS_DAMPING}}
      <p class="caption">Fig. 5: Comparison of undamped (constant) and lightly damped (decaying) total energy. The shaded region represents energy dissipated to the surroundings.</p>
