  </div>

  <h3>Key Observations from the Graphs</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Energy conservation:</strong> The sum E<sub>k</sub> + E<sub>p</sub> is constant at every displacement &mdash; the horizontal line in Fig. 1.</li>
    <li><strong>Phase relationship:</strong> When E<sub>k</sub> is maximum (at equilibrium, x = 0), E<sub>p</sub> is zero, and vice versa at the turning points.</li>
    <li><strong>Frequency doubling:</strong> Both E<sub>k</sub> and E<sub>p</sub> complete two cycles per oscillation period because each passes through a maximum twice per cycle.</li>
    <li><strong>Velocity&ndash;displacement ellipse:</strong> The area enclosed by the ellipse in the v&ndash;x plane is proportional to the total energy of the system.</li>
    <li><strong>Damping:</strong> In a real system, energy is dissipated to the surroundings (e.g. air resistance, internal friction), causing the amplitude and total energy to decrease over time.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--  DAMPING & RESONANCE                                         -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<section>
  <h2>6. Damping, Forced Oscillations and Resonance</h2>

  <h3>3.1 Light, Critical and Heavy Damping</h3>
  <p>The degree of damping determines how quickly the oscillation amplitude decays:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Light damping:</strong> Amplitude decays exponentially over many oscillations. The system continues to oscillate but with decreasing energy.</li>
    <li><strong>Critical damping:</strong> The system returns to equilibrium in the shortest possible time without oscillating. Used in car suspension systems and galvanometer design.</li>
    <li><strong>Heavy (over)damping:</strong> The system returns to equilibrium slowly without oscillating.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3.2 Resonance and Energy Transfer</h3>
  <p>When a driving frequency matches the natural frequency of the system, resonance occurs. At resonance:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The amplitude of oscillation reaches a maximum.</li>
    <li>Energy transfer from the driver to the oscillator is most efficient.</li>
    <li>The width of the resonance curve depends on the degree of damping: lighter damping gives a sharper, taller peak.</li>
    <li>At resonance, the driving force is in phase with the velocity, so work done by the driving force is always positive &mdash; energy is fed into the system each cycle.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3.3 Practical Examples of Resonance</h3>
  <table class="data-table" style="min-width:85%;">
    <tr><th>System</th><th>Driving frequency</th><th>Natural frequency</th><th>Observation</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Microwave oven</td><td>2.45 GHz</td><td>H<sub>2</sub>O molecular vibration</td><td>Water molecules absorb energy; food heats</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pendulum clock</td><td>Escapement mechanism</td><td>Pendulum period</td><td>Amplitude maintained against friction</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Bridge (e.g. Tacoma Narrows)</td><td>Wind-induced vortices</td><td>Bridge structural mode</td><td>Large-amplitude oscillations &rarr; collapse</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Radio receiver</td><td>Incoming radio wave</td><td>LC circuit resonance</td><td>Selective amplification of target frequency</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Musical instrument</td><td>Vibrating string/air column</td><td>Standing wave mode</td><td>Sound amplification at harmonics</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>3.4 Effect of Damping on the Resonance Curve</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>As damping <strong>increases</strong>, the resonance peak amplitude <strong>decreases</strong>.</li>
    <li>The resonance curve becomes <strong>broader</strong> (less sharp).</li>
    <li>The resonance frequency shifts <strong>slightly lower</strong>.</li>
    <li>For very heavy damping, the resonance peak disappears entirely (the system is overdamped).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These relationships are illustrated in Fig. 8.</p>
</section>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--  WORKED EXAMPLE                                              -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<section>
  <h2>7. Worked Example</h2>

  <div class="example-box">
    <p class="given"><strong>Problem:</strong> A 200 g mass oscillates on a spring of spring constant 50 N m<sup>&minus;1</sup> with amplitude 4.0 cm. Determine:</p>
    <p class="given">(a) the total mechanical energy of the system;<br>
    (b) the speed of the mass when the displacement is 2.0 cm from equilibrium;<br>
    (c) the kinetic energy and potential energy at this displacement.</p>

    <p><strong>Solution:</strong></p>

    <p><strong>(a)</strong> At maximum displacement, all energy is potential:</p>
    <p>E<sub>total</sub> = &frac12;kx<sub>0</sub>&sup2; = &frac12; &times; 50 &times; (0.040)&sup2; = &frac12; &times; 50 &times; 1.6 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup></p>
    <p><strong>E<sub>total</sub> = 4.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;2</sup> J = 40 mJ</strong></p>

    <p><strong>(b)</strong> Use v = &plusmn;&omega;&radic;(x<sub>0</sub>&sup2; &minus; x&sup2;). First find &omega;:</p>
    <p>&omega; = &radic;(k/m) = &radic;(50 / 0.200) = &radic;250 = 15.81 rad s<sup>&minus;1</sup></p>
    <p>At x = 0.020 m:</p>
    <p>v = 15.81 &times; &radic;(0.040&sup2; &minus; 0.020&sup2;) = 15.81 &times; &radic;(1.2 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup>)</p>
    <p>v = 15.81 &times; 0.03464 = <strong>0.548 m s<sup>&minus;1</sup></strong></p>

    <p><strong>(c)</strong> At x = 0.020 m:</p>
    <p>E<sub>p</sub> = &frac12;kx&sup2; = &frac12; &times; 50 &times; (0.020)&sup2; = <strong>1.0 &times; 10<sup>&minus;2</sup> J = 10 mJ</strong></p>
    <p>E<sub>k</sub> = E<sub>total</sub> &minus; E<sub>p</sub> = 40 &minus; 10 = <strong>30 mJ</strong></p>
    <p>(Check: E<sub>k</sub> = &frac12;mv&sup2; = &frac12; &times; 0.200 &times; 0.548&sup2; = 0.0300 J = 30 mJ &check;)</p>
  </div>
</section>

