<!--  CONCEPT CASE STUDIES                                        -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<section>
  <h2>9. Concept Case Studies &mdash; Cambridge-Style Reasoning</h2>
  <p style="font-size:0.9rem;color:#636c76;margin-bottom:0.6rem;">Each case study tests your ability to apply SHM concepts to novel real-world contexts, following CIE 9702 syllabus criteria. Marks and reasoning steps follow Cambridge convention (M1/A1/B1).</p>

  <!-- Case Study A -->
  <div class="question">
    <p class="q-header">Case Study A &mdash; Suspension Bridge Oscillations [6]</p>
    <p class="q-context">A suspension bridge has a natural frequency of oscillation of 0.25 Hz. On a windy day, gusts of wind produce a periodic driving force on the bridge deck at frequencies between 0.10 Hz and 0.50 Hz.</p>

    <div class="q-part"><p><span class="cmd-word">(a) Identify</span> the condition required for resonance to occur. <span class="marks">[1]</span></p></div>
    <div class="q-part"><p><span class="cmd-word">(b) Explain</span> why the bridge oscillations would be most dangerous when the wind gust frequency is 0.25 Hz. Use the concept of energy transfer from the driving force to the oscillator. <span class="marks">[3]</span></p></div>
    <div class="q-part"><p><span class="cmd-word">(c) Suggest</span> and explain one method engineers could use to reduce the amplitude of the bridge oscillations. <span class="marks">[2]</span></p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="mark-scheme">
    <p class="ms-header">Mark Scheme &mdash; Case Study A</p>
    <div class="ms-point"><strong>(a)</strong> B1: Resonance occurs when the driving frequency equals (or is close to) the natural frequency of the bridge. [1]</div>
    <div class="ms-point"><strong>(b)</strong> M1: At resonance, the driving frequency equals the natural frequency (0.25 Hz). A1: The driving force is in phase with the velocity of the bridge, so work done by the force is positive throughout each cycle &mdash; energy is continuously fed into the system. A1: The amplitude builds up to a maximum, potentially exceeding the structural limits of the bridge and causing damage or collapse. [3]</div>
    <div class="ms-point"><strong>(c)</strong> M1: Adding dampers (shock absorbers) or tuned mass dampers increases the damping of the bridge structure. A1: Increased damping dissipates energy from the oscillating system, reducing the amplitude at resonance and broadening the resonance curve so that the bridge is less sensitive to any specific driving frequency. [2]</div>
    <p class="ms-total">Total: [6]</p>
  </div>

  <!-- Case Study B -->
  <div class="question">
    <p class="q-header">Case Study B &mdash; Designing a Seismometer [6]</p>
    <p class="q-context">A seismometer contains a mass&ndash;spring system designed to detect ground vibrations. The mass is 0.50 kg and the spring constant is adjustable. A seismometer must be critically damped so that it returns to equilibrium quickly after a disturbance.</p>

    <div class="q-part"><p><span class="cmd-word">(a) Calculate</span> the spring constant <em>k</em> required for the seismometer to have a natural period of 2.0 s. <span class="marks">[2]</span></p></div>
    <div class="q-part"><p><span class="cmd-word">(b) Explain</span> the meaning of critical damping and why it is desirable in a seismometer rather than light or heavy damping. <span class="marks">[2]</span></p></div>
    <div class="q-part"><p><span class="cmd-word">(c) The</span> seismometer is taken to a location where <em>g</em> is 1% smaller. Would the period change? Justify your answer. <span class="marks">[2]</span></p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="mark-scheme">
    <p class="ms-header">Mark Scheme &mdash; Case Study B</p>
    <div class="ms-point"><strong>(a)</strong> M1: T = 2&pi;&radic;(m/k) &rarr; k = 4&pi;&sup2;m/T&sup2;. A1: k = 4&pi;&sup2; &times; 0.50 / 4.0 = 4.93 N m<sup>&minus;1</sup> (accept 4.9&ndash;5.0 N m<sup>&minus;1</sup>). [2]</div>
    <div class="ms-point"><strong>(b)</strong> M1: Critical damping returns the system to equilibrium in the shortest possible time without oscillating. A1: Light damping would overshoot (oscillate), giving a delayed reading. Heavy damping would return too slowly, missing rapid successive tremors. Critical damping gives the fastest accurate reading. [2]</div>
    <div class="ms-point"><strong>(c)</strong> M1: The period of a mass&ndash;spring system is T = 2&pi;&radic;(m/k), which does NOT contain <em>g</em>. A1: Therefore, the period remains unchanged. (A pendulum-based seismometer <em>would</em> be affected, but this mass&ndash;spring system is gravity-independent.) [2]</div>
    <p class="ms-total">Total: [6]</p>
  </div>

  <!-- Case Study C -->
  <div class="question">
    <p class="q-header">Case Study C &mdash; Microwave Oven Resonance [5]</p>
    <p class="q-context">A microwave oven operates at 2.45 GHz to heat food. Water molecules in the food have a natural resonance frequency near this value. A student argues that the oven works by forcing the water molecules to oscillate at 2.45 GHz.</p>

    <div class="q-part"><p><span class="cmd-word">(a) Explain</span> why the microwaves cause water molecules to gain energy efficiently. Refer to the SHM concept of resonance. <span class="marks">[2]</span></p></div>
    <div class="q-part"><p><span class="cmd-word">(b) Discuss</span> whether the student's statement that molecules are "forced to oscillate at 2.45 GHz" is accurate. What actually happens to the molecules? <span class="marks">[2]</span></p></div>
    <div class="q-part"><p><span class="cmd-word">(c) Suggest</span> why microwave oven doors have a metal mesh with holes smaller than the wavelength of 2.45 GHz radiation. <span class="marks">[1]</span></p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="mark-scheme">
    <p class="ms-header">Mark Scheme &mdash; Case Study C</p>
    <div class="ms-point"><strong>(a)</strong> M1: The driving frequency (2.45 GHz) closely matches the natural resonance frequency of water molecules. A1: At resonance, energy transfer from the microwaves to the molecules is most efficient &mdash; the oscillating electric field does maximum work on the polar water molecules, increasing their kinetic (rotational) energy, which is experienced as heating. [2]</div>
    <div class="ms-point"><strong>(b)</strong> M1: The statement is partially inaccurate. The molecules are not forced to oscillate at 2.45 GHz; they oscillate (rotate) at their own natural frequency, but the driving field at 2.45 GHz most effectively pumps energy into this oscillation. A1: The molecules absorb energy and increase their rotational kinetic energy (temperature), which is then shared via collisions with neighbouring molecules. [2]</div>
    <div class="ms-point"><strong>(c)</strong> B1: The mesh acts as a Faraday cage &mdash; the holes are smaller than the wavelength (~12.2 cm), so the microwaves cannot pass through, but visible light can. [1]</div>
    <p class="ms-total">Total: [5]</p>
  </div>

  <!-- Case Study D -->
  <div class="question">
    <p class="q-header">Case Study D &mdash; Car Suspension System [5]</p>
    <p class="q-context">A car's suspension system uses springs and shock absorbers (dampers) to provide a comfortable ride. Each wheel has a spring of spring constant 2.0 &times; 10<sup>4</sup> N m<sup>&minus;1</sup> supporting a quarter of the car's mass (approximately 300 kg).</p>

    <div class="q-part"><p><span class="cmd-word">(a) Calculate</span> the natural frequency of the car body (mass&ndash;spring system). <span class="marks">[2]</span></p></div>
    <div class="q-part"><p><span class="cmd-word">(b) Explain</span> why the shock absorber is designed to provide critical damping rather than light damping. <span class="marks">[2]</span></p></div>
    <div class="q-part"><p><span class="cmd-word">(c) Predict</span> what would happen if the spring corrodes and its spring constant decreases. How would the ride quality change? <span class="marks">[1]</span></p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="mark-scheme">
    <p class="ms-header">Mark Scheme &mdash; Case Study D</p>
    <div class="ms-point"><strong>(a)</strong> M1: f = (1/2&pi;)&radic;(k/m) = (1/2&pi;)&radic;(2.0 &times; 10<sup>4</sup> / 300). A1: f = (1/2&pi;)&radic;(66.7) = 1.30 Hz (accept 1.3 Hz). Period T = 0.77 s. [2]</div>
    <div class="ms-point"><strong>(b)</strong> M1: Light damping would cause the car to oscillate (bounce repeatedly) after hitting a bump, leading to an uncomfortable ride and poor tyre contact. A1: Critical damping returns the car body to equilibrium in the shortest time without oscillation, giving a smooth ride and rapid tyre recovery. [2]</div>
    <div class="ms-point"><strong>(c)</strong> B1: A lower k reduces the natural frequency, making the ride feel softer/floatier and increasing the tendency to oscillate at lower bump frequencies. [1]</div>
    <p class="ms-total">Total: [5]</p>
  </div>

</section>

